From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kenny Root <kroot@google.com>
Date: Thu, 12 May 2016 14:15:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 17/32] Use SSL_session_reused to check when a session was
 reused

The returned session_id could be exactly the same in the case of TLS
session tickets, so use the SSL_session_reused API to determine exactly
when a session was reused.

(cherry picked from commit 1115fa0f6dbbff3a913fbce39ca98f9a78425c72)

Bug: 28751153
Change-Id: Ie82e4d1bb326d7e7deb7981a1e57df393f6c0e1f
(cherry picked from commit 91f9851e8703ba93d3c9bfbac5c139f84e4c44dd)
(cherry picked from commit 3f575a65c1d159358ec8484421fd0e72274ca01e)

Android 4.2 port (android-4.2.2_r1) of patch 0017 of the 4.4 series,
made from its Android 4.3 port. Paths and names: libcore/crypto of 4.4
(org.conscrypt, org_conscrypt_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacrypto) is
libcore/luni on 4.2 (org.apache.harmony.xnet.provider.jsse,
org_apache_harmony_xnet_provider_jsse_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacore).
Android 4.2 NativeCrypto passes native pointers as Java int:
SSL_session_reused(int), a jint C parameter, (I)Z in the JNI table. NULL
instead of nullptr (C++98), as on 4.3. 4.2 has no sessionToReuse
(libcore 5911a70, Android 4.3): after the handshake it looked the
session up by its id in the session context. That lookup misses when
OpenSSL 1.0.1u gives a resumed session a new id (the server issued a new
session ticket), and a resumed handshake then created a new session, or
threw IllegalStateException with session creation disabled. This patch
therefore also takes the OpenSSLSocketImpl part of 5911a70 that 4.3 has:
the client keeps the session it offers in a local and takes it over when
SSL_session_reused() is true; a server, and a client that offered
nothing, create a new session, as on 4.3 and 4.4. The rest of 5911a70
(AbstractSessionContext, createCertChain, tests) is not taken.
